<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Section 1.9: Combining Functions</title>

    <p>
        <alert>MTH 124</alert>
    </p>
    <page>
    <p>
        The first activity is a repeat from last worksheet:
    </p>
    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.9.1</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> defined by the
                table below and the piecewise linear functions <m>p</m> and
                <m>q</m> defined by the graph below.
                Assume that the lines in the figure pass through whole number
                coordinates where they appear to do so; for example,
                <m>(2,2)</m> lies on the graph of <m>q</m>, and <m>(3,-3)</m>
                lies on the graph of <m>p</m>.
            </p>

            <sidebyside widths="40% 30%">
                <tabular>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>10</cell>
                        <cell>15</cell>
                        <cell>20</cell>
                        <cell>25</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
                        <cell>9</cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>

                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        The graphs of p and q for you to combine.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-9-1">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="yes"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-4,4),(-3,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,-1),(-3,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,-1),(3.5,-4))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <label anchor="(-4,2.)" alignment="e">
                                    <m>
                                        p
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <line endpoints="((-4,-4),(-2,4))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <line endpoints="((4,1),(-2,4))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <label anchor="(-3,-2.)" alignment="s">
                                    <m>
                                        q
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>h(x) = f(x)+g(x)</m>.
                    Determine <m>h(3)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>h(3)=f(3)+g(3)=20+2=22</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>r(x) = p(x) -q(x)</m>.
                    Determine <m>r(-1)</m> exactly.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>r(-1) = p(-1)-q(-1)=0.25-3.5=3.25</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Are there any values of <m>x</m> for which <m>r(x) = 0</m>?
                    If not, explain why; if so, determine all such values, with
                    justification.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    If <m>r(x)=0</m>, then <m>p(x)-q(x)=0</m>, or in other words 
                    <m>p(x)=q(x)</m>.  This only happens at <m>x=-2.75</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>k(x) = f(x) \cdot g(x)</m>.
                    Determine <m>k(0)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>k(0)=f(0)\cdot g(0) = (5)(9) = 45</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>s(x) = \frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</m>.
                    Determine <m>s(1)</m> exactly.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>s(1) = \frac{p(1)}{q(1)}=\frac{-0.5}{2.5} = -\frac15</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Are there any values of <m>x</m> in the interval
                    <m>-4\le x \le 4</m> for which <m>s(x)</m> is not defined?
                    If not, explain why; if so, determine all such values, with
                    justification.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    If <m>s(x)=\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</m> is not defined, either <m>p</m>
                    is not defined, <m>q</m> is not defined, or <m>q(x)=0</m> because you 
                    cannot divide by 0.  The only possibility here is that <m>q(x)=0</m> 
                    when <m>x=-3</m>, so <m>s(x)</m> is undefined at <m>x=-3</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>
    </page>
    
    <page>

    <definition xml:id="def-sum-functions">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The <term>sum of f and g</term> is the function 
                            <m>f+g</m> defined by <m>{(f+g)(x) = f(x)+g(x)}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The <term>difference of f and g</term> is the function 
                            <m>f-g</m> defined by <m>{(f-g)(x) = f(x)-g(x)}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The <term>product of f and g</term> is the function 
                            <m>f\cdot g</m> defined by <m>{(f\cdot g)(x) = f(x)\cdot g(x)}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The <term>quotient of f and g</term> is the function 
                            <m>\frac{f}{g}</m> defined by <m>{(\frac{f}{g})(x) = \frac{f(x)}{g(x)}}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.9.2</title>
        <introduction>
         
        
        <p>Consider the functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> defined by 
        the following figures. 
        </p>
        <sidebyside widths = "35% 35%">
            <image >
            <shortdescription>A piecewise graph f(x).</shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-9-2">
                <diagram dimensions="(300,180)" margins="5">
                  <definition>g(x)=1/sqrt(x)</definition>
                    <coordinates bbox="(-3,-2,4,5)">
                        <grid-axes ylabel="f(x)"/>
                       <line endpoints="((-3,4),(-1,3))" stroke="blue" />
                       <point p="(-1,3)" fill="white"/>
                       <line endpoints="((-1,1),(1,4))" stroke="blue" />
                       <point p="(-1,1)" fill="blue"/>
                       <point p="(1,4)" fill="blue"/>
                       <line endpoints="((1,1),(5,-2))" stroke="blue" />
                       <point p="(1,1)" fill="white"/>
                       <point p="(2,0.25)" fill="white"/>
                       
                    </coordinates>
                </diagram>
            </prefigure>
           </image>

                      <image width="35%">
            <shortdescription>A piecewise graph g(x).</shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-9-3">
                <diagram dimensions="(300,180)" margins="5">
                  <definition>g(x)=-x^2+4</definition>
                    <coordinates bbox="(-3,-2,4,5)">
                        <grid-axes ylabel="g(x)"/>
                        <graph function="g" domain="(-3,1)"/>
                       <point p="(1,3)" fill="blue"/>
                       <point p="(0,1)" fill="blue"/>
                       <point p="(0,4)" fill="white"/>
                       <line endpoints="((1,2),(4,-1))" stroke="blue" />
                       <point p="(1,2)" fill="white"/>
                       <point p="(2,1)" fill="white"/>
                       
                    </coordinates>
                </diagram>
            </prefigure>
           </image>

        </sidebyside>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the value of <m>(f+g)(0)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>(f+g)(0)=f(0)+g(0)=2.5+1=3.5</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the value of <m>(g-f)(1)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution  component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>g(1)-f(1)=3-4=-1</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the value of <m>(f\cdot g)(-1)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>f(-1)g(-1)=(1)(3)=3</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Are there any values of <m>x</m> for 
                    which <m>(\frac{f}{g})(x)</m> is undefined?  
                    If not, explain why.  If so, determine the values.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution  component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    For this quotient to be undefined, either <m>f</m> needs to be undefined, 
                    <m>g</m> needs to be undefined, or <m>g(x)=0</m> since we cannot divide by 0.  
                    Both <m>f(x), g(x)</m> are undefined at <m>x=2</m> and only that x-value. 
                    But <m>g(x)=0</m> at <m>x=-2, 3</m>.   So <m>(\frac{f}{g})(x)</m> is undefined
                    at <m>x=-2,2,3</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For what values of <m>x</m> is <m>(f\cdot g)(x) = 0</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution  component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    If <m>f(x)g(x)=0</m> then either <m>f(x)=0</m> or <m>g(x)=0</m>, 
                    that is the only way the product is 0.  The first happens at 
                    <m>x\approx 2.25</m>, and the second happens at <m>x=-2, 3</m>.  
                    So here, this occurs when <m>x=2.25, -2, 3</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Are there any values of <m>x</m> for which <m>(f-g)(x) = 0</m>?  Why or why not?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution  component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    Hard to tell because they are not on the same graph.  This happens if 
                    <m>f(x)-g(x) = 0</m>, that is if <m>f(x) = g(x)</m>.  They look to be 
                    the same at about <m>(0.6, 3.5)</m>, though it is hard to say for sure. 
                    So at <m>x=0.6</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

    </activity>
</page>
<page>
    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.9.3</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m>f</m> be a function that measures a car's fuel
                 economy in the following way. Given an input 
                 velocity <m>v</m> in miles per hour, <m>f(v)</m> is
                  the number of gallons of fuel that the car consumes
                   per mile (i.e., “gallons per mile”). We know that
                   <m>f(60) = 0.04</m>.

            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the meaning of the statement “<m>f(60)=0.04</m>” in the context 
                    of the problem? That is, what does this say about 
                    the car's fuel economy? Write a complete sentence to 
                    explain.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution  component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    The input is velocity, the output is fuel consumption, so this means that 
                    at a speed of 60 mph your car consumes 0.04 gallons per mile.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the function <m>g(v)=\frac{1}{f(v)}</m>.  What is the value of 
                    <m>g(60)</m>?  What are the units on <m>g</m>?  What does <m>g</m> measure?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution  component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    So <m>g(60)=\frac{1}{f(60)} = \frac{1}{0.04} = 25</m>.  The units are 
                    1 / (gallons / mile), which is miles per gallon.  So <m>g(v)</m> 
                    measures the car's miles per gallon that it gets when going a velocity of 
                    <m>v</m> mph.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the function <m>h(v)=v\cdot f(v)</m>.  What is the value of 
                    <m>h(60)</m>?  What are the units on <m>h</m>?  What does <m>h</m> measure?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution  component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    So <m>h(60) = 60 \cdot f(60) = 60 (0.04) = 2.4</m>.  It has units of 
                    <m>(\frac{miles}{hour})(\frac{gallons}{mile}) = \frac{gallons}{hour}</m>, 
                    so it measures the gallons of gas per hour the car uses when going a 
                    velocity <m>v</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Do <m>f(60), g(60), h(60)</m> tell us fundamentally different information, or are they all essentially saying the same thing? Explain.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution  component="S1-9-soln">
                <p>
                    They're all essentially the same thing, just a different view. They all
                    tell us about the car's fuel consumption (in either gallons per mile, miles per gallon, 
                    or gallons per hour) when the car goes 60 mph.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
    </activity>
    </page>
</handout>